    "result_text": "Full suite passes \u2014 **exit 0, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the rule: **reject creating an owner when the owner's city already contains 50 or more owners, responding 409.**\n\nFollowing the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions, I added:\n\n1. **`RequireOwnerCityCapacity`** (`function/owner/`) \u2014 a pipeline step that counts existing owners whose city matches case-insensitively (the same match used by `AssignCustomerCode`'s per-city sequence) and throws when the count is \u2265 50. It runs before `SaveOwner`, so the count excludes the owner being created.\n\n2. **`OwnerCityCapacityExceededException`** (checked, `extends Exception`) \u2014 carries the offending city.\n\n3. **`OwnerCityCapacityExceededExceptionHandler`** \u2014 responds `409 CONFLICT` with body `{\"errors\":[\"city\"]}`, mirroring the existing household/telephone handlers.\n\n4. **Escalation YAML** mapping the exception to its handler.\n\n5. **Wired the step** into `owners.POST.yml` as `cityCapacity`, after the household check and before `build`, with `govern: [ transaction ]` like its neighbours.\n\n6. **`Cp19Tests`** \u2014 a boundary test asserting 409 at 50 owners and acceptance at 49, using the base's `fillCity` helper (whose comment already anticipated a \"per-city cap\" rule).\n\nAll existing tests plus the two new ones pass.",
